{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"dac91d04e28d9c41","name":"card_event_parachute_assault","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/OceaniaStorm/events/card_event_parachute_assault","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"PARACHUTE ASSAULT","text":"Fill your support line with 506th AIRBORNE. Give them Blitz and Shock.","flavorText":"US parachute tactics during WWII focused on surprise, disruption, and seizure of key terrain behind enemy lines. Paratroopers were dropped ahead of the main ground or amphibious force, often at night, with specific objectives such as bridges, road junctions, causeways, artillery positions, and routes needed by advancing troops. In Normandy, for example, the 82nd and 101st Airborne were dropped behind Utah Beach to secure exits from the beachhead and hinder German movements. Once on the ground, units were expected to assemble quickly, attack their objectives, disrupt enemy communications and reinforcements, and then hold until relieved. Because parachute drops frequently scattered units, small groups often had to improvise and continue toward objectives without their full formations. Heavy weapons, artillery, engineers, jeeps, and additional infantry followed in gliders.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_parachute_assault","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_parachute_assault_title","SourceString":"PARACHUTE ASSAULT","LocalizedString":"PARACHUTE ASSAULT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_parachute_assault_text","SourceString":"Fill your support line with 506th AIRBORNE. Give them Blitz and Shock.","LocalizedString":"Fill your support line with 506th AIRBORNE. Give them Blitz and Shock."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_parachute_assault_history","SourceString":"US parachute tactics d